'''Cynthia L. Holscher''' ({{Née|'''Blumhorst'''}}, born April 26, 1969)<ref name="votesmart">{{cite web |title=Cindy Holscher's Biography |url=https://justfacts.votesmart.org/candidate/biography/171793/cindy-holscher |website=Vote Smart |access-date=June 21, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.linkedin.com/posts/glenn-blumhorst-0a614a2_honored-to-join-my-sister-cindy-holscher-activity-7285773495744036866-ivls|title=Honored to join my sister, Cindy Holscher (third from left), as she took the oath of office for her second term as a Kansas state senator. &#124; Glenn Blumhorst|website=www.linkedin.com}}</ref> is an American politician who represented the 16th district in the [[Kansas House of Representatives]] from 2017 to 2020<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kslegislature.org/li_2018/b2017_18/members/rep_holscher_cindy_1/ |title=Representative Cindy Holscher |publisher=Kslegislature.org |access-date=January 14, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|author=Tim Carpenter |url=https://www.cjonline.com/state-government/news/2017-08-10/kansas-democrat-shares-ideas-after-spurning-run-governor |title=Kansas Democrat shares ideas after spurning run for governor |publisher=Cjonline.com |date=August 10, 2017 |access-date=January 14, 2019}}</ref> and has represented the 8th district in the [[Kansas Senate]] since 2021.<ref name="ballotpedia">{{cite web |title=Cindy Holscher |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Cindy_Holscher |website=Ballotpedia |access-date=June 21, 2020}}</ref> She is a member of the [[Democratic Party of the United States|Democratic Party]] and is the Democratic nominee in the [[2026 Kansas gubernatorial election]].<ref name="Smith 2026">{{cite |last=Smith |first=Mitch |title=Cindy Holscher Wins Kansas Democratic Primary for Governor |date=August 5, 2026 |issn=0362-4331 |website=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/us/elections/kansas-democratic-primary-governor.html |language=en-US |access-date=August 6, 2026 |page=}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Harvel |first1=Jack |title=Likely Democratic voters polled on who they'd vote for for governor |url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/state/2026/01/15/kansas-sen-cindy-holscher-holds-the-lead-in-polling-over-ethan-corson/88184633007/?gnt-cfr=1&gca-cat=p&gca-uir=false&gca-epti=z11xx94p116950c116950v11xx94&gca-ft=210&gca-ds=sophi |access-date=March 6, 2026 |publisher=Topeka Capital-Journal |date=January 15, 2026}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Holscher was raised on a farm near [[Slater, Missouri]] and graduating from Slater High School in 1987. Her father, Archie, was a union concrete construction worker, and her mother, Wanita, worked as a bus driver and school custodian. Holscher graduated from the [[University of Missouri–Columbia]] in 1992 with degrees in journalism and political science. She is a first-generation college graduate.<ref name="votesmart"/><ref name="Carpenter 2025">{{cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Tim |title=Kansas senator leans into bipartisan allure in campaign for Kansas governor |website=Kansas Reflector |date=October 6, 2025 |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2025/10/06/kansas-senator-leans-into-bipartisan-allure-in-campaign-for-kansas-governor/ |access-date=September 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="Barnes 2025">{{cite web |last=Barnes |first=Whitney |title=Slater native to run for Kansas governor |website=Marshall Democrat-News |date=June 19, 2025 |url=https://www.marshallnews.com/slater-native-to-run-for-kansas-governor/ |access-date=September 11, 2026}}</ref><ref name="LegiStorm">{{cite web |title=Kansas State Sen. Cindy Holscher |website=LegiStorm |url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/263784/Cindy_Holscher.html |access-date=June 29, 2026}}</ref><ref name="Pfleger-Bernat 2026">{{cite web |last=Pfleger-Bernat |first=Josh |title=Kansas governor candidates bring the heat during debate at State Fair |website=The Sunflower |date=September 12, 2026 |url=https://thesunflower.com/109675/news/kansas-governor-candidates-bring-the-heat-during-debate-at-state-fair/ |access-date=September 15, 2026}}</ref>

==Career==
===Professional career===
Holscher spent most of her career in corporate management at [[Sprint Corporation|Sprint]]. She also worked as a temporary substitute teacher in [[Johnson County, Kansas|Johnson County]] schools.<ref name="Carpenter 2026">{{cite web |last=Carpenter |first=Tim |title=Kansas Democratic gubernatorial candidate's union membership sparks political finger-pointing |website=HPPR |date=June 25, 2026 |url=https://www.hppr.org/hppr-news/2026-06-25/kansas-democratic-gubernatorial-candidates-union-membership-sparks-political-finger-pointing |access-date=June 29, 2026}}</ref>

===Political career===
In 2016, Holscher was elected to represent District 16 in the Kansas House of Representatives, defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Amanda Grosserode]]. She was reelected in 2018.<ref name="ballotpedia" />

In 2020, Holscher was elected to represent district 8 in the Kansas Senate, defeating Republican state representative [[James Todd (Kansas politician)|James Todd]] in a competitive race.<ref name="ballotpedia" />

====2026 gubernatorial campaign====
{{main|2026 Kansas gubernatorial election}}

On June 12, 2025, Holscher became the first Democrat to announce a bid for the [[List of governors of Kansas|governor of Kansas]] in the [[2026 Kansas gubernatorial election]].<ref>{{cite journal|url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2025/06/12/cindy-holscher-is-first-major-kansas-democrat-in-2026-governors-race/84153137007/|title=Cindy Holscher is first major Kansas Democrat to declare for 2026 governor race|author=Jason Altadidd|date=June 12, 2025|journal=The Topeka Capital-Journal|access-date=June 12, 2025}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://kansasreflector.com/2025/06/12/democrat-steps-forward-in-race-for-kansas-governor-focuses-on-schools-and-kitchen-table-issues/|title=Democrat steps forward in race for Kansas governor, focuses on schools and 'kitchen table' issues |first=Sherman|last=Smith|date=June 12, 2025|website=Kansas Reflector}}</ref> On May 14, 2026, she announced that Wichita state representative [[Kelechi Ohaebosim]] would be her running mate.<ref>{{cite journal|url=https://kansasreflector.com/2026/05/14/democratic-ticket-in-kansas-governors-race-pairs-legislators-from-johnson-sedgwick-counties/|title=Cindy Holscher announces running mate for 2026 governor race|author=Tim Carpenter|date=May 14, 2026|journal=Kansas Reflector|access-date=May 14, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://kansasreflector.com/2026/05/14/democratic-ticket-in-kansas-governor's-race-pairs-legislators-from-Johnson-Sedgwick-counties/|title=Democratic ticket in Kansas governor's race pairs legislators from Johnson, Sedgwick counties |first=Tim|last=Carpenter|date=May 14, 2026|website=Kansas Reflector}}</ref>

Holscher's campaign has focused on [[cost of living]] and [[public education]].<ref name=":0" /> She supports [[Legalization of non-medical cannabis in the United States|cannabis legalization]] for both [[Medical cannabis|medical]] and recreational uses.<ref>{{Cite news |date=July 16, 2026 |title=What the Kansas governor candidates say about the prospect of legalizing marijuana |url=https://www.kctv5.com/2026/07/16/what-kansas-governor-candidates-say-about-prospect-legalizing-marijuana/ |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[KCTV5]]}}</ref> Holscher has said that tax revenue from cannabis legalization could be put toward public education<ref>{{Cite news |date=July 21, 2026 |title=What the candidates for Kansas governor said about their plans for public school funding |url=https://www.kctv5.com/2026/07/21/what-candidates-kansas-governor-said-about-their-plans-public-school-funding/ |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[KCTV5]]}}</ref> and that Kansas farmers could profit from cultivating cannabis.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |date=July 15, 2026 |title=How the candidates for Kansas governor say they plan to help farmers |url=https://www.kctv5.com/2026/07/15/how-candidates-kansas-governor-say-they-plan-help-farmers/ |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[KCTV5]]}}</ref> She seeks a statewide moratorium on [[data center]]s.<ref>Timothy Cama, E&E Politico, August 5, 2026 "Data center foes win big in Tuesday’s primaries - Winners this week either want to crack down on new data centers or enact a moratorium" https://www.eenews.net/articles/data-center-foes-win-big-in-tuesdays-primaries/</ref> Holscher is critical of [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|Donald Trump's tariffs]] and their impact on farmers.<ref name=":1" /> She supports [[Medicaid expansion]] in Kansas.<ref>{{Cite news |date=July 15, 2026 |title=What Kansas gubernatorial candidates will do to address the rural healthcare crisis |url=https://www.kctv5.com/2026/07/15/what-kansas-gubernatorial-candidates-will-do-address-rural-healthcare-crisis/ |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[KCTV5]] |quote=Cindy Holscher: Certainly. I do see a pathway for it. And I will tell you, actually, I worked with a bipartisan group back in 2017. We got Medicaid expansion passed then. Then-Governor Brownback vetoed it. I believe we fell just a couple of votes short of overriding that. But since that time period, extremist leadership has blocked us from moving it forward.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=July 15, 2026 |title=What the Kansas gubernatorial candidates said about their plan to address the economy |url=https://www.kctv5.com/2026/07/15/what-kansas-gubernatorial-candidates-said-about-their-plan-address-economy/ |access-date=July 31, 2026 |work=[[KCTV5]]}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Holscher married Greg Holscher in September 1992.<ref name="IG 2026">{{Cite Instagram |user=cindyforkansas |postid=DOOmlvJkdjA |date=September 5, 2025 |title=Today is our wedding anniversary! I'm so grateful for a life full of love, adventure, & our beautiful children with my husband Greg. He's my partner, my other half, and I'm lucky to love him ❤️ Donate $33 today at cindyforkansas.com to help us celebrate 33 years of marriage! |access-date=June 28, 2026}}</ref> They have three children together.<ref name="votesmart"/>

